   3.94,
   3.81,
   3.66,
   3.61
  ],
  "kinds": [
   "text",
   "text",
   "text",
   "text",
   "text",
   "text"
  ],
  "answer": "Chapter 3 notes that sovereign debt in emerging market and developing economies has risen sharply – from under 40 % of GDP in 2010 to over 70 % in recent years – and that this increase has pushed debt-service costs higher (from 6 % of government revenues in 2010 to an estimated 11 % in 2025)[S1].  The chapter finds that higher debt-to-GDP ratios are positively linked to both dollar-denominated sovereign spreads and domestic-currency bond yields, with the effect becoming larger as debt levels rise; since 2010, the rise in EMDE debt-to-GDP has been associated with roughly 110 basis points higher sovereign spreads and about 30 basis points higher domestic-currency yields[S3].",
  "latency": 4.0,
  "timings": {
   "embed": 0.1,
